    "---\n",
    "## 🧭 Part 5: VECTOR Architectural Reflection & Self-Evaluation\n",
    "**Weight: 40% of Weekly Score | Fixed Role Target: V4-E4-C4-T4-O5-R4**\n",
    "\n",
    "Evaluate your implementation against the official 6 VECTOR dimensions. Document your rationale and architectural evidence below:\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1. Vision Clarity [V] (Target Level 4)\n",
    "- **Architectural Judgment**: Unlike naive passthrough, this design checks encoded and plain-text inputs before they reach the model, isolates accepted content as untrusted data, and validates model output before returning it. These layers reduce risk but cannot guarantee safety on their own; adversarial testing, monitoring, and human oversight remain necessary.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2. Edge Judgment [E] (Target Level 4)\n",
    "- **Decision Window & Logging**: The sanitizer blocks suspicious decoded Base64/Hex payloads first; threat heuristics then block direct injection, persona, and jailbreak indicators before any API call. The agent records stage, outcome, and reason in `audit_log` without retaining raw user text. Production deployments should forward appropriately minimized events to an access-controlled SIEM.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3. Context Fluency [C] (Target Level 4)\n",
    "- **Multi-Encoding & Stream Inspection**: The sanitizer inspects raw text and decodes bounded Base64/Hex candidates, applying the same threat signatures to each stream. Accepted original text is HTML-escaped before XML wrapping; this detects common encodings, but additional normalization and testing are needed for other encodings and evasion techniques.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 4. Trust Architecture [T] (Target Level 4)\n",
    "- **Zero-Trust & Delimiter Isolation**: User content is treated as data, escaped so it cannot close the `<user_input>` tag, and accompanied by system instructions not to follow directives inside that boundary. XML delimiters are a defense-in-depth aid, not a hard security boundary, so input checks and output validation remain essential.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 5. Orchestration Intelligence [O] (Target Level 5)\n",
    "- **Pipeline Decomposition**: The sequence is: validate input; inspect raw text and scan/decode bounded Base64/Hex candidates; block obfuscation or raw-text indicators; recheck with threat heuristics; escape and wrap accepted text in `<user_input>`; call the model with the dedicated system mission; validate generated output; return only safe output; record the outcome in the audit log.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 6. Range [R] (Target Level 4)\n",
    "- **Enterprise & Program Alignment**: This layer can provide evidence for documented security controls, but it does not itself establish SOC 2 compliance. Production integration should add managed secrets, least-privilege access, rate limiting, bounded retries, retention controls, SIEM tracing, alerting, and regular red-team/evaluation cycles. The same validated boundary can precede later RAG retrieval and tool execution, with access checks applied to retrieved data and actions.\n"
   ]
  }
 ],
